    'marcus-chen',
    'External SSDs have replaced hard drives for portable storage: no moving parts means shock resistance, and modern NVMe-in-enclosure designs reach speeds that make internal drives feel slow by comparison. The five here separate on speed, durability, and price.\n\nThe Samsung T7 at $89 for 1TB wins overall as the balanced pick. Read speeds around 1,050MB/s and write speeds near 1,000MB/s handle 4K video editing directly from the drive, and its pocketable aluminum body shrugs off drops from two meters. Samsung\'s reliability track record across the T-series is the strongest in the category. It is the drive to buy if you want no compromises worth noticing at a mainstream price.\n\nThe SanDisk Extreme at $129 adds ruggedization: IP55 dust and water resistance, a silicone shell, and drop protection to three meters. Field photographers and travelers who toss drives in bags deserve the insurance. Speed matches the T7.\n\nThe Crucial X9 at $59 for 1TB is the budget pick that skips nothing essential: around 1,050MB/s reads, solid build, and Micron NAND underneath. It lacks the T7\'s metal body and extreme-scenario durability but costs a third less.\n\nThe WD My Passport at $99 wins capacity per dollar at higher tiers — 4TB and 5TB versions are the practical archive options, with 1,050MB/s-class performance. Backup hoarders and video libraries justify the size.\n\nThe LaCie Rugged at $149 is the professional pick: IP67 rating (full water submersion survival), a five-year warranty with data recovery services included, and consistent performance under sustained writes — the drive for working professionals whose data cannot be lost.\n\nMatch speed to your use: 1,000MB/s-class drives saturate USB 3.2 Gen 2; faster drives need Thunderbolt ports to show their advantage. For plain backup, the Crucial X9 is the smart money; for field work, the SanDisk or LaCie; for pro workflows, the T7.',
